<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of Glamorgan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of Glamorgan is a fertile lowland region of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> and Bridgend, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel coast. After the Norman conquest of Glamorgan around 1093 its manors were granted to the knights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon">Robert Fitzhamon</a>, who built castles and churches there, while Welsh lords kept the uplands. It is now a county borough.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan.jpg" alt="Vale of Glamorgan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeremy Segrott from Cardiff, Wales, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nash_Point,_Vale_of_Glamorgan,_south_Wales_(26088435901).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
